---
title: Styling and Interactions
slug: devlink/styling-and-interactions
description: Understanding how styling and interactions are preserved in DevLink components
'og:title': Styling and Interactions
'og:description': Understanding how styling and interactions are preserved in DevLink components
subtitle: Perserve your design system's appearance and behavior in your app
---
This guide will help you understand how DevLink preserves the styles of your design system and the interactions of your Webflow components in your app.

## Styles

Add your global styles to your application to ensure that your Webflow components are styled consistently across your app.

 <CodeBlocks>
        ```jsx title="Next.js 13"
        // layout.tsx
        import '@/devlink/global.css';
        ```
        ```jsx title="Older versions of Next.js"
        // _app.tsx
        import "@/devlink/global.css";
        ```
</CodeBlocks>

DevLink uses a combination of CSS Modules and global styles to maintain the look and feel of your Webflow components:

<AccordionGroup>
  <Accordion title="CSS Modules for component styles">
    Each component exported through DevLink gets its own CSS Module file with component-specific styles that:

      - Isolate component styles to prevent global CSS conflicts
      - Preserve Webflow styling details including media queries
      - Maintain responsive behavior
      - Support special selectors like `:global()` for Webflow classes

    ```css title="navbar.module.css" maxLines=10
    .navbar-component {
      display: flex;
      min-height: 80px;
      padding-right: 2rem;
      padding-left: 2rem;
      align-items: center;
      background-color: transparent;
    }

    .navbar-container {
      display: flex;
      width: 96%;
      height: 80px;
      max-width: 1280px;
      margin-right: auto;
      margin-left: auto;
      justify-content: space-between;
      align-items: center;
    }
    ```

    <Note>
      When working with custom HTML IDs in components, see the [Custom IDs and selectors](#custom-ids-and-selectors) section below.
    </Note>
  </Accordion>

  <Accordion title="Global styles">
    The `global.css` file contains styles that apply across all components:

    - [Normalize.css](https://github.com/necolas/normalize.css) resets for browser consistency
    - Webflow's base styles (e.g. `.w-slider` and `.w-nav-link`)
    - Font imports from your Webflow project
    - [CSS Variables](https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/33961268146323-Variables) for colors and other properties
    - Default element styling and form element resets
    - Custom CSS tag selectors from your site (e.g. "All Links" or "Body (All Pages)" style selectors)

    <Tip>
      For global styling that should affect all components, use the "Body (all pages)" tag selector in Webflow rather than setting classes directly on the body element, as the latter won't be included in `global.css`.
    </Tip>

    ```css title="global.css"
    /* 1. Font imports from Webflow */
    @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');

    /* 2. Normalize.css resets - browser consistency */
    html {line-height:1.15;-webkit-text-size-adjust:100%} body {margin:0}
    /* More normalize.css rules... */

    /* 3. Color swatches as CSS variables */
    :root {
      --primary-color: hsla(210, 100%, 56%, 1.00);
      --secondary-color: hsla(258, 100%, 56%, 1.00);
      /* More color variables from your Webflow project */
    }

    /* 4. Webflow-specific classes for components */
    .w-slider { position: relative; }
    .w-nav-link { position: relative; display: inline-block; }
    /* More Webflow component classes... */

    /* 5. Default element styling */
    body {
      font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
      color: #333;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    a {
      font-weight: 700;
      text-decoration: underline;
    }
    ```

  </Accordion>
</AccordionGroup>

 ### Advanced configuration
 If you need to customize the styles of your Webflow components, you can configure the following options:

    1. **CSS modules**<br/>
    By default, DevLink uses CSS modules to isolate component styles. If needed, you can disable this with:

        ```js title=".webflowrc.js"
        module.exports = {
          cssModules: false
        }
        ```

    2. **Tag selectors**<br/>
    To minimize global styling and only include the bare essentials:

        ```js title=".webflowrc.js"
        module.exports = {
          skipTagSelectors: true
        }
        ```

        <Note>
          When using `skipTagSelectors: true`, ensure parent elements of DevLink components have defined heights/widths, or set `height: 100%` on your HTML and body elements to allow proper rendering of navigation components.
        </Note>

## Interactions

[Webflow interactions](https://webflow.com/interactions-animations) are JavaScript-powered animations and transitions. DevLink preserves these interactions by wrapping them in a special provider component:
{/* <!-- vale off --> */}
<CodeBlocks>
```jsx title="Next.js 13"
// layout.tsx
import { DevLinkProvider } from '@/devlink/DevLinkProvider';
import '@/devlink/global.css';

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <DevLinkProvider>
          {children}
        </DevLinkProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

```jsx title="Older versions of Next.js"
// _app.tsx
import "@/devlink/global.css";
import { DevLinkProvider } from "@/devlink/DevLinkProvider";
import type { AppProps } from "next/app";
export default function App({ Component, pageProps }: AppProps) {
  return (
    <DevLinkProvider>
      <Component {...pageProps} />
    </DevLinkProvider>
  );
}
```
</CodeBlocks>
{/* <!-- vale on --> */}

<Note>
  The DevLinkProvider must wrap your application for Webflow interactions to work. Webflow Cloud apps can handle this automatically if you set `provider: true` in your DevLink configuration.
</Note>

### Page interactions
Webflow components support [page triggers](https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/33961357722643-Triggers-and-animations#page-triggers) with a limitation: DevLink only exports the first page interaction. If a component uses multiple page interactions across different pages, DevLink will only export the first one.

## Advanced styling

### Custom IDs and selectors

When you add [custom HTML IDs](https://webflow.com/glossary/id-selectors) to your Webflow components, DevLink automatically transforms them using CSS Modules. This transformation helps keep your component styles organized and prevents any conflicts between different parts of your application. The transformed IDs follow a specific pattern that combines your component name with your custom ID:

**`<ComponentName>_<custom-id>__<unique-identifier>`**

For example, if you add a custom ID to a Grid element within a `Hero` component named `featured-section`, DevLink will transform it into a unique identifier like `Hero_featured-section__abc123` in the generated code.

To target these elements with CSS, use wildcard selectors that match the component name and custom ID pattern:

```css
[id*="Hero_featured-section__"] { 
  /* your styles */ 
}
```

<Frame caption="Custom ID">
  <img src="file:54a5e03f-688a-474e-a9a4-15346b50b5ef" alt="Custom ID" width="50%" />
</Frame>

### Dynamic IDs and styling hooks

Some Webflow elements use HTML IDs as styling hooks, including Grid and Quick Stack components. These IDs are visually indicated by pink markers in the Style Panel.

When attempting to make these IDs dynamic using component properties, you'll encounter a technical limitation. DevLink can't simultaneously preserve the required CSS styling references while supporting dynamic ID values. Here's why:

```jsx
// ❌ Problematic approach - will break styling
import { Grid } from '@/devlink/components/grid';

function DynamicSection({ sectionId }) {
  return (
    <Grid id={sectionId}/>
  );
}
```

The issue arises because Webflow generates CSS that directly references these IDs:

```css
/* Generated Webflow CSS */
#grid-123 {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
  /* Other grid styles... */
}
```

When you make the ID dynamic, the CSS selector no longer matches, breaking the styling.

**Recommended solution: Custom attributes**

Instead of modifying IDs, use custom attributes for dynamic values. This preserves Webflow's styling while allowing for dynamic identification:

```jsx
// ✅ Recommended approach
import { Grid } from '@/devlink/components/grid';

function DynamicSection({ sectionId }) {
  return (
    <Grid 
      data-section-id={sectionId} 
      // Original Webflow ID remains unchanged
      id="grid-wrapper-123"
    />
  );
}
```

products/webflow-cloud/pages/devlink/pro.markdown:
---
title: Using props and slots in DevLink Components
slug: devlink/props
description: How props work between Webflow and React components
'og:description': How props work between Webflow and React components
'og:title': Using Props in DevLink Components
subtitle: Use props to customize Webflow components in React
---
DevLink converts Webflow's [component properties](https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/33961219350547-Component-properties) into fully typed React props, giving you programmatic control over your components. These props come with full TypeScript support and preserve all the defaults and rules set up in Webflow. You can use them to update content, handle user interactions, and control how your components behave in your React app.

## Props

DevLink components support two types of props that enable you to customize and extend Webflow components in your React application:

- **Basic props**: Webflow's native component properties for content and visibility. The visual editor defines these properties, and DevLink automatically types them in React.
- **Runtime props**: React-specific features like event handlers and overrides for custom styling.

<Note>Components generated from DevLink are all exported with their own declaration file (`.d.ts`) for type safety</Note>

## Basic props

Basic props are the most common type of prop to use in DevLink components. These types of props are typically typed when exported and Webflow knows how to handle them (see [component properties](https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/33961219350547-Component-properties#supported-properties) for list).

- **text**: Set dynamic text content for elements like buttons, headings, or paragraphs. See more about text and content props in the [Webflow help center](https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/33961219350547-Component-properties#content-properties).
  ```jsx
  <Button text="Click me" />
  ```
- **visibility**: Control the visibility of elements or icons within a component. See more about visibility props in the [Webflow help center](https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/33961219350547-Component-properties#visibility-properties).
  ```jsx
  <Button showIcon={isLoggedIn} />
  ```

**Example: Notification button**

This Button component has text and visibility props to show the number of new notifications for a user. The `text` prop sets the number of notifications, while the `showThis` visibility prop shows/hides the button based on the number of notifications. The `buttonProps` prop is a runtime prop to handle the click event of the button. We'll cover runtime props in the next section.


```tsx title="Dashboard.tsx"
import { Button } from "../../devlink/Button";


export default function Dashboard({numNotifications}: {numNotifications: number}) {
  return (
    <div>
      <Button
        text={`${numNotifications} new notifications`}
        showIcon={numNotifications > 0}
        buttonProps={{
          onClick: () => handleClick()
        }}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Runtime props

Runtime props extend DevLink components with React-specific functionality that isn't natively supported in Webflow. When you add a runtime props property to an element, the DevLink compiler automatically adds a corresponding prop to your component and spreads it onto the underlying HTML element. Create runtime props to manage:

- event handling
- set specific inline styles
- override the component class (note: this will make the element lose any pre-existing classes defined in Webflow so this isn't recommended unless absolutely necessary)
- [React refs](https://react.dev/learn/referencing-values-with-refs)

### Creating runtime props in Webflow
Add runtime props to your components by selecting a supported element, then navigating to the element's settings panel. In the DevLink section, you can add runtime props to the element. Only certain elements have support for Runtime Props.

<Frame>
  <img src="file:8e171b3f-9ef8-4833-822b-0ce444ae1ad3" alt="Runtime props in Webflow" />
</Frame>

### Using runtime props in your React component

DevLink passes runtime props as an object to the component as a prop with the same name as the runtime prop. For example, if you add a `buttonProps` runtime prop to a button, you can access it in your component as `buttonProps`.

{/* <!-- vale off --> */}
<CodeBlocks>
```jsx title="Event Handler"
import { Button } from "../../devlink/Button";
import { handleClick } from "./utils";


export default function Dashboard({numNotifications}: {numNotifications: number}) {
  return (
    <div>
      <Button
        text={`${numNotifications} new notifications`}
        showIcon={numNotifications > 0}
        buttonProps={{
          onClick: () => handleClick()
        }}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

```jsx title="Inline Styles"
import { Button } from "../../devlink/Button";


export default function Dashboard({numNotifications}: {numNotifications: number}) {
  return (
    <div>
      <Button
        text={`${numNotifications} new notifications`}
        showIcon={numNotifications > 0}
        buttonProps={{style: {backgroundColor: "e7ffe6"}}}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

```jsx title="React Ref"
import { Button } from "../../devlink/Button";
import { useRef } from "react";


export default function Dashboard({numNotifications}: {numNotifications: number}) {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);
  return (
    <div>
      <Button
        text={`${numNotifications} new notifications`}
        showIcon={numNotifications > 0}
        buttonProps={{ ref: inputRef }}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```
</CodeBlocks>
{/* <!-- vale on --> */}
## Slots
A slot is a DevLink-specific property that takes a component as its value. Slots enable you to nest components inside of components within your React project. This is useful when you have custom React components that can't be built natively in Webflow (for example, if the components rely on third-party libraries or need support for custom logic), but you want to nest them in a Webflow-made component.

Then, you can pass components into the slot property of the React component. For instance, if you have a logo component and a div component with a `logoSlot` slot property, you can write `logoSlot = {<logo / >}` to nest the logo inside of your div component.

To set a slot property on an element, create a wrapper `div` block in the component, then go to the Element settings panel > DevLink > Slot, then connect it to a property.

<Warning title="DevLink slots are different than Webflow Component Slots">
  DevLink slots enable you to inject custom React components or content into specific areas of an exported React component. In contrast, Webflow Component Slots are used within Webflow's visual editor to insert Webflow Components into designated areas of a component. We understand this distinction can be confusing. Webflow Component Slots aren't supported in DevLink.
</Warning>

